    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 0 failures).\n\n## What I changed\n\nThe membership level is derived at read time in `OwnerMapper.membershipLevel(Owner)` (`src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java`), wired into the `OwnerDto` via the `@Mapping(target = \"membershipLevel\", ...)` expression.\n\nPreviously the method summed base + email + `namesakeCount == 0` and hard-capped the result at 3, so **level 4 was simply unreachable** \u2014 the \"reserved for tenure\" comment was never actually implemented.\n\nI made level 4 a real, tenure-gated tier:\n\n- Added a **household factor**: +1 when the owner belongs to a household (has a `householdId`). Combined with the email and no-namesake bonuses on top of the base 1, the non-tenure factors now sum to 4.\n- Added a **tenure gate**: the level is capped at 3 unless the owner's tenure (`ChronoUnit.DAYS.between(registrationDate, today)`) is **more than 365 days**, in which case the cap lifts to 4. A new helper `tenureDays(Owner)` returns 0 when no registration date is stored (or it's in the future), so an owner with no recorded tenure can never qualify.\n\nThis makes the spec literally true: a newly created owner has zero tenure \u2192 capped at 3. The acceptance test (`coreNewOwnerCappedAtLevel3`) creates an owner with an email, a postcode (which yields a `householdId`) and a unique name (`namesakeCount == 0`) \u2014 raw factors sum to 4, but with zero tenure it correctly lands at **level 3, not 4**, exactly as the parenthetical describes.\n\nOne interpretation note worth flagging: the mapper operates on a single `Owner` and has no repository access, so I implemented the household factor as \"belongs to a household\" (`householdId` present) rather than literally counting 3 members. The spec's \"3-member household\" reads as an illustration of the maximal pre-tenure owner, and there's no test distinguishing household *size* \u2014 but if the intent was actual member-counting, that would require a different design (passing a count into the mapper). Let me know if you'd prefer that.",
