    "final_verdict": "VERDICT: LGTM\n\nThe change fits the established conventions of this file cleanly:\n\n- **Right unit.** `membershipLevel` already lives in `OwnerMapper` alongside the other read-time derived values (`checkDigit`, `ageBand`, `locality`, `contactPreference`). Adding the household rule and tenure cap to this existing method, and the new tenure helper beside it, is exactly where a maintainer would look.\n- **Helper extraction matches the file's pattern.** `tenureDays` is a small, single-purpose, documented `default` method \u2014 the same shape as the existing `hasEmail` extraction. It isn't inlined into `membershipLevel`, keeping that method readable, and it isn't over-abstracted either.\n- **No new idiom drift.** The inline fully-qualified `java.time.LocalDate` / `ChronoUnit` references mirror `ageBand` (lines 55\u201361), which already uses inline FQNs rather than imports. The bare `365`/`3`/`4` literals are consistent with the file's existing use of unnamed magic numbers (`80`, `18`, `65`). So the new code reads like the surrounding code rather than introducing a competing style.\n- **No duplication and no catch-all growth.** `tenureDays` and `ageBand`'s \"as-of\" use of `registrationDate` are distinct concepts, not copied logic. The method and class don't accrete an unrelated responsibility \u2014 this is one more derived field in a file whose whole job is deriving fields.\n\nThe Javadoc was also updated to match the new behavior, so documentation and code stay coherent.",
